WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.540
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.870
Today, we're not going to have the usual visual
fireworks that we had with most topics in

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:15.750
computer graphics, but I really hope you'll
still find this episode enjoyable and stimulating.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.950
This episode is also going to be a bit heavy
on what optimization is and we'll talk a little

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.970
bit at the end about the intuition of the
paper itself.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.700
We are going to talk about mathematical optimization.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.090
This term is not to be confused with the word
"optimization" that we use in our everyday

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:40.340
lives for, for instance, improving the efficiency
of a computer code or a workflow.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:46.150
This kind of optimization means finding one,
hopefully optimal solution from a set of possible

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.800
candidate solutions.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.170
An optimization problem is given the following
way: one, there is a set of variables we can

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:59.460
play with, and two, there is an objective
function that we wish to minimize or maximize.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.670
Well, this probably sounds great for mathematicians,
but for everyone else, maybe this is a bit

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.670
confusing.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.290
Let's build a better understanding of this
concept through an example!

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:15.330
For instance, let's imagine that we have to
cook a meal for our friends from a given set

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.580
of ingredients.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:22.380
The question is, how much salt, vegetables
and meat goes into the pan.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.450
These are our variables that we can play with,
and the goal is to choose the optimal amount

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.290
of these ingredients to maximize the tastiness
of the meal.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:37.420
Tastiness will be our objective function,
and for a moment, we shall pretend that tastiness

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.740
is an objective measure of a meal.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.479
This was just one toy example, but the list
of applications is endless.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:50.700
In fact, optimization is so incredibly ubiquitous,
there is hardly any field of science where

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.770
some form of it is not used to solve difficult
problems.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:02:00.240
For instance, if we have the plan of a bridge,
we can ask it to tell us the minimal amount

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.970
of building materials we need to build it
in a way that it remains stable.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:10.389
We can also optimize the layout of the bridge
itself to make sure the inner tension and

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.500
compression forces line up well.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.329
A big part of deep learning is actually also
an optimization problem.

00:02:17.329 --> 00:02:22.629
There are a given set of neurons, and the
variables are when they should be activated,

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:27.480
and we're fiddling with these variables to
minimize the output error, which can be, for

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:34.670
instance, our accuracy in guessing whether
a picture depicts a muffin or a chihuahua.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:41.280
The question for almost any problem is usually
not whether it can be formulated as an optimization

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.659
problem, but whether it is worth it.

00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:48.400
And by worth it I mean the question whether
we can solve it quickly and reliably.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.689
An optimizer is a technique that is able to
solve these optimization problems and offer

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:57.019
us a hopefully satisfactory solution to them.

00:02:57.019 --> 00:03:01.709
There are many algorithms that excel at solving
problems of different complexities, but what

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:08.229
ties them together is that they are usually
handcrafted techniques written by really smart mathematicians.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.900
Gradient descent is one of the simplest optimization
algorithms where we change each of the variables

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:19.480
around a bit, and as a result, see if the
objective function changes favorably.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.949
After finding a direction that leads to the
most favorable changes, we shall continue

00:03:23.949 --> 00:03:26.079
our journey in that direction.

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:28.150
What does this mean in practice?

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.760
Intuitively, in our cooking example, after
making several meals, we would ask our guests

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.680
about the tastiness of these meals.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:42.209
From their responses, we would recognize that
adding a bit more salt led to very favorable

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:48.069
results, and since these people are notorious
meat eaters, decreasing the amount of vegetables

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:52.519
and increasing the meat content also led to
favorable reviews.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:57.690
And we, of course, on the back of this newfound
knowledge, will cook more with these variable

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.809
changes in pursuit of the best possible meal
in the history of mankind.

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:07.199
This is something that is reasonably close
to what gradient descent is in mathematics.

00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:13.249
A slightly more sophisticated version of gradient
descent is also a very popular way of training

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:14.680
neural networks.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.670
If you have any questions regarding the gradient
part, we had an extended Two Minute Papers

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:25.130
episode on what gradients are and how to use
them to build an awesome algorithm for light

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.130
transport.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.020
It is available, where?

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.880
Well, of course, in the video description
box, Károly, why are you even asking.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.580
So what about the paper part?

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:41.290
This incredible new work of Google DeepMind
shows that an optimization algorithm itself

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.950
can emerge as a result of learning.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:50.090
An algorithm itself is not considered the
same one thing as deciding what an image depicts

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:55.720
or how we should grade a student essay, it
is an algorithm, a sequence of steps we have

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:56.720
to take.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.270
If we're talking about outputting sequences,
we'll definitely need to use a recurrent neural

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:02.550
network for that.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.900
Their proposed learning algorithm can create
new optimization techniques that outperform

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:13.010
previously existing methods not everywhere,
but on a set of specialized problems.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:17.840
I hope you've enjoyed the journey, we'll talk
quite a bit about optimization in the future,

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:18.840
you'll love it.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.760
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
